  "scenario_narratives": {
    "base": "Mid-cycle Chevron at a $70 Brent deck: the Hess deal gives it a 30% Stabroek interest that grows through the decade, TCO's FGP/WPMP run at capacity, and the Permian plateau at ~1.7m boe/d throws off cash at lower capex. Production grows 2-3%/yr, margins gain ~1pp from synergies and efficiency, and ~$19-21bn of annual owner FCF funds the dividend plus mid-range buybacks.",
    "bull": "A structurally higher mid-cycle ($80) after the 2026 Hormuz shock exposes a decade of upstream underinvestment, with Chevron's low-breakeven barrels (Permian, Tengiz, Guyana) capturing it. Venezuela's September-2026 terms add barrels without much capital, and the Microsoft/Project Kilby power deal opens a contracted gas-to-power business that supports a higher terminal multiple.",
    "bear": "The spike reverses: de-escalation plus OPEC+ spare capacity push Brent to a $60 mid-cycle. Chevron's specific geopolitical concentrations bite together: CPC transit through Russia constrains TCO, OFAC pulls the Venezuela license again, and the Essequibo dispute clouds non-operated Guyana. Refining normalises and energy-transition demand erosion caps terminal growth near zero."
  },
  "valuation_philosophy": {
    "summary": "Chevron is valued on mid-cycle owner FCF at a $70 Brent deck, not on the spike-inflated H1-2026 run-rate. The current price discounts something close to the bull deck, so on a through-the-cycle view the stock is pricing in the Hormuz shock persisting. The H2-2026 windfall is credited once in the bridge; it does not justify capitalising spike cash flows.",
    "key_risks_to_valuation": [
      "Brent deck: each $1/bbl is ~$0.6bn/yr after-tax cash flow, roughly $3-4/share of value over the horizon at 8%; the scenario decks span $60-$80.",
      "Terminal assumptions for a depleting resource base: the 10-12x cap and 1.5% terminal growth assume reserve replacement continues; an energy-transition decline would make terminal growth negative.",
      "Geopolitical concentration: TCO exports via the Russian-transit CPC line, Venezuela OFAC license volatility, the Guyana-Venezuela Essequibo dispute.",
      "Equity-affiliate cash (TCO, CPChem) is only captured as distributed; TCO distribution policy and loan repayments can shift year-to-year cash materially.",
      "The H2-2026 windfall bridge item ($5bn) is an estimate from company sensitivity, not a reported figure."
    ]
  },
