    "notes": "MODEL: owner-FCF DCF, component build, MID-CYCLE (cyclical commodity producer; routed via dcf-methods SKILL.md row 'Operating company, non-US or cyclical' + owner-fcf-adaptations.md). First valuation of CVX; no memory entry. WHY MID-CYCLE: the valuation date sits inside a Brent spike (H1-26 avg $92, >$107 in September on the Iran/Hormuz disruption); Q2-26 NI $12.2bn is the highest in six years and is not a repeatable base. Company sensitivity is ~$600m after-tax earnings/cash flow per $1/bbl Brent (Q2-26 10-Q), so the H1-26 run-rate (~$50bn OCF annualised) de-rates to ~$37bn OCF at $70, i.e. ~$18-19bn FCF after $18-21bn guided capex -- consistent with FY24 ($15.0bn at $81 Brent, pre-Hess) and FY25 ($16.6bn at $69, half-year Hess). FY0 revenue $195bn = FY25 $189bn at $69 Brent + full-year Hess + 7-10% guided 2026 production growth, held to a $70 deck. Year-1 owner FCF ~$18.9bn (9.3% margin). The above-deck cash already being earned in H2-2026 is added once in the equity bridge, not capitalised. BALANCE SHEET (10-Q 2026-06-30): cash $8,527m + time deposits $3m = $8,530m; debt = short-term $401m + long-term incl. current and finance leases $36,674m = $37,075m; net debt $28,545m (FY25 10-K: $34,461m). INTEREST INCOME not stripped: CVX is net-debt, and flows are unlevered with net debt deducted. EQUITY AFFILIATES (TCO 50%, CPChem 50%): their cash contribution arrives through OCF as distributions and is inside the FCF base and margin calibration; affiliate capex is outside CVX capex. BUYBACKS: repurchaser (flat count funded, not a convention).",
